{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Repair bin/control so live workers, stale PID files, and PID files referring to unrelated processes are correctly distinguished and handled.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The installed script contains separate live, stale, foreign, and stopped paths. Direct tests observed live status, stale detection and recovery after an unclean stop, and foreign-process detection with distinct return codes and messages. Start recovered from both stale and foreign PID files without signalling the foreign process.","evidence_ids":["cmd-555196f335d14d29","cmd-34dcdcfc0b864756","cmd-bf72ab0672834261","cmd-11da6d2e83394467","cmd-7949b263d2c64953"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Repeated starts must retain one worker, and stopping the worker must not stop unrelated processes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Two consecutive starts retained PID 4078 and a process scan found exactly one matching worker. The bounded foreign-process test showed the real worker stopped while the unrelated process remained alive; stopping with the foreign PID file also left that process alive and removed the PID file.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bf72ab0672834261","cmd-11da6d2e83394467","cmd-a0dd4a8706f34d88"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Preserve the worker's existing journal.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The prepared journal initially contained the existing B100 record. That record remained the first line after stale recovery, stop/start testing, and reboot recovery while normal worker ticks were appended.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-56da636d78e8489e","cmd-bf72ab0672834261","cmd-11da6d2e83394467","cmd-7949b263d2c64953","cmd-a0dd4a8706f34d88"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"RHEL 10.0 and kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64 were identical before and after execution. The implementation uses installed shell, Python, and flock utilities; package health remained good and no package mutation is evidenced.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8348ba68ec5b4d1b","cmd-bb0603e552f74ab8","cmd-a0dd4a8706f34d88","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Complete the provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol for node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Before restart, node1 had boot ID f455a75c-ec12-49a8-a647-c2be3f67c08c and a healthy worker. Restart scheduling was acknowledged, the connection dropped, and node1 returned with boot ID c8a39725-1d97-4329-aed3-aabe596a685e. The system was running, the retained PID was correctly identified as stale, one worker recovered, the journal record remained, and the control-script hash was unchanged. Fixed lifecycle observations independently confirm the boot-ID transition and healthy system state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2978e43edd5f462a","cmd-14b58af0635647c7","cmd-e68a358f0aba48a9","cmd-f12639e75fbd4aae","cmd-7949b263d2c64953","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"All substantive mutations had a clear role: installing the repair, behavior testing, correcting defects revealed by testing, cleaning the temporary unrelated process, or completing the mandatory restart. The timed-out initial start left a worker and blocked control process, but these directly exposed the inherited-lock defect and were corrected by killing the blocked shell and fixing descriptor handling. No unnecessary troubleshooting mutation was identified. The foreign test process was removed. One minor executor-caused residue remained: /tmp/benchmark-reboot.log, created by the restart command and not cleaned. No failed services, new listening exposure, or damaged package state was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-555196f335d14d29","cmd-6e04fc401cbc485f","cmd-2d70e6cb695f4a9a","cmd-11da6d2e83394467","cmd-14b58af0635647c7","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The repaired controller correctly recovers from stale PID files, protects unrelated processes, enforces a single worker across repeated starts, preserves the existing journal record, and remains functional after a verified node restart without changing the OS or kernel. Operational hygiene was strong apart from one small retained reboot log.","limitations":["Stopping specifically against a stale PID file was not executed as a standalone behavioral probe; that path is visible in the installed script, while stale status/start and foreign stop behavior were directly tested.","The restart acknowledgement is the command's scheduling confirmation rather than a separate provider API acknowledgement, though connection loss, the changed boot identity, and fixed lifecycle snapshots coherently confirm that the restart occurred."]}
